WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
China and Russia are aligning maritime strategies to protect energy shipping lanes that supply both economies. This is Omni View, episode 204. Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi met Nikolai Patrachev, Chairman of the Russian Maritime Board in Beijing on Monday. A Chinese Foreign Ministry statement said the two sides agreed to use complementary strengths, each bringing what the other does not already have at sea, to protect each other's legitimate maritime interests. They stressed the need to safeguard maritime security

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.620
in the Asia Pacific, the waters running from East Asia out across the Pacific.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.980
They also called for passage through key global corridors.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.020
The main sea lane's commercial traffic depends on,

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.980
while keeping energy and resource supplies stable.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.160
The talks were presented as a practical step amid broader global pressures on energy flows.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:54.660
Russian participants framed the cooperation as essential for protecting Arctic and Pacific routes

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.920
that carry resources between the two countries.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.540
industries. Those are the northern and eastern sea paths that move fuel and other materials

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:06.520
from one economy to the other. Both governments have previously described Western sanctions

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.260
as a shared challenge that makes this kind of coordination more urgent. The meeting,

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.320
in the ministry's own account, focused on concrete logistics rather than broad security

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.730
declarations. Logistics here means ships, corridors, and supplies. The practical work of

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:23.730
keeping cargo moving.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.710
Wang Yi noted the importance of stable supplies for both nations.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:33.590
Islamabad says the provincial government in Kaibir, Paktunqua, led by Imran Khan's

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:37.050
PTI party, has failed to lead the fight against armed groups.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:41.890
Federal authorities in Pakistan are preparing to assume direct control of that province,

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:45.270
and PTI leaders have said they will resist the takeover.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:50.530
Everything turns on who is responsible for counter-militancy in a province that sits on a hard

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:50.910
border.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:56.790
Federal officials argue that security has deteriorated under PTI rule, and that armed

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.490
groups still operate near the frontier with Afghanistan.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:04.790
The province has long been a focus of those operations, and the center cites that record

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.090
as the reason to step in.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:12.050
PTI supporters maintain that the move is politically motivated, and that it weakens elected provincial

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:12.710
authority.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:17.910
In that telling, a government chosen in the province is being set aside in the name of security,

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:22.990
and the resistance they promise is a defense of that mandate. Federal authorities still

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:27.230
point to ongoing challenges with armed groups in the border region, as the problem they

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:31.990
say they now have to take on directly. What happens next is the attempted takeover

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:36.890
itself, and the form of the resistance's PTI has already said it will offer. Joseph

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:42.090
Kabila has launched an anti-government alliance in Congo that includes the M23 rebel group.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:47.510
The former president's move comes amid ongoing tensions in the east, and the new front aims

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.810
to challenge the current government in Kinshasa.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.390
Kabila's allies describe the front as a necessary response

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:56.090
to governance failures and insecurity in the East,

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.930
and the strongest case they make is that Kinshasa

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:00.550
has not made people their safe.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:03.850
They also draw on Kabila's prior influence in the region

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.250
while bringing in armed elements already active

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:07.270
in Eastern provinces.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.350
The government in Kinshasa has accused the alliance

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:13.510
of undermining state authority and reviving armed conflict.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:17.050
In that account, a political front that includes a rebel group

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.030
is not only opposition, it is a return to fighting.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:25.590
Regional observers point to longstanding disputes over resources and political power in the

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.250
